hello and welcome to med simplified i'm  dr umar malik and in this video we will  talk about the best foods that you can  include in your diet  if you have gout if you haven't watched  our videos on joint pain  i'm going to leave the links in the  description box down below so you can  check them out  gout is a very common disease and its  prevalence has been increasing in the  recent years  it is a painful disease affecting one  joint at a time  most commonly the big toe joint pain in  the joints is known as  arthellatria and joint pain or  arthelasia  is as common as a flu and can be caused  by a wide range of reasons  such as bad posture overuse  and misuse of joints like in case of  athletes such as footballers sprinters  and tennis players  it can also develop due to diseases like  osteoarthritis  rheumatoid arthritis many neurological  problems  kidney and liver problems and gout we  have made  separate videos on most of these topics  so you can check out these videos by  clicking the links in the description  box down below  well goud is a type of arthritis an  inflammatory condition of the joints  it affects an estimated 8.3 million  people in the united states alone  people with gout experience sudden and  severe  attacks of pain swelling and  inflammation in the joints  nearly half of the gout cases affect the  big toes  while other cases affect the fingers  wrists  knees and heels gout symptoms or attacks  occur when there is too much uric acid  in the blood  uric acid is actually a waste product  made by the body when it digests certain  foods  when uric acid levels are high it can  accumulate  crystallize and then settle in these  joints  this process triggers swelling  inflammation and intense pain in the  joints  now the question you might ask is how  does our diet  affect gout if you have gout certain  foods may trigger an attack by raising  your uric acid levels  this is because these trigger foods are  commonly high in purines  purines are actually substances found  naturally in certain foods  when your body digests purines it makes  uric acid as a waste product  normally this is not a concern for  healthy people as they efficiently  remove excess uric acid from the body  however people with gout cannot  remove this excess uric acid from the  blood and thus  a high purine diet may lead to  accumulation of uric acid in the joints  which leads to a gout attack research  has shown  that restricting high purine foods and  taking the appropriate medication can  prevent gout attacks  and improve your quality of life foods  that commonly trigger gout attacks  include organ meats red meats seafood  alcohol and beer they contain a moderate  to high amount of humanes  however there is one exception to this  rule research shows that high purine  vegetables do not trigger cow attacks  interestingly fructose and sugary  beverages can increase the risk of gout  and gout attacks  even though they are not purine rich for  instance  a study including over 125 000  participants  found that people who consumed the most  fructose had a 62 percent higher risk of  developing out  so to summarize if you are susceptible  to gout attacks  avoid the main culprits which are high  purine foods  these are foods that contain more than  200 milligrams of purines  per 100 grams you should also avoid high  fructose foods  as they may trigger a count attack here  are  a few major high purine foods and high  fructose foods  that you should avoid all types of meats  which include liver and kidney meats  also  fishes like herring trout mackerel tuna  sardines haddock and more  seafood like scallops crabs and shrimps  sugary beverages especially fruit juices  and sugary sodas  foods with added sugars like honey agave  nectar and high fructose corn syrup  yeasts like nutritional yeasts brewer  yeasts  and other yeast supplements additionally  refined carbs like white breads cakes  and cookies should also be avoided  although they are not high in purines or  fructose they are low in nutrients and  may raise your uric acid levels  now you know what to avoid naturally you  may ask  what foods you should include in your  diet if you have gout  although a girl friendly diet eliminates  many foods  there are still plenty of low purine  foods you can enjoy  foods are considered low purine when  they have less than 100 milligram of  purines per 100 grams  here are some low purine foods that are  generally safe for people with cow  all fruits are generally fine for gout  cherries may even help prevent attacks  of gout by lowering uric acid levels  and reducing inflammation all vegetables  are also fine  including potatoes peas mushrooms  eggplants  and dark green leafy vegetables legumes  are  also good including lentils beans  soybeans and tofu  nuts and seeds are also very good for  people with cow  whole grains like oats brown rice and  barley  all dairy products are safe and low fat  dairy appears to be especially  beneficial for people with cow  eggs are also good beverages like coffee  tea and green tea have no harmful  effects  all herbs and spices are also good  plant-based oils  are also good including canola oil  coconut oil  olive oil and flax oils apart from your  diet there are several lifestyle changes  that can help you  lower your risk of gout and gout attacks  let's look at a few of them  number one is to lose weight if you have  gout  carrying excess weight can increase your  risk of gout attacks  this is because excess weight can make  you more resistant to insulin  leading to insulin resistance in these  cases the body cannot use  insulin properly to remove sugars from  the blood  this not only increases your risk of  diabetes but insulin resistance  also promotes high uric acid levels  research shows that losing weight can  help reduce  insulin resistance and lower uric acid  levels  that said you need to be careful  dietitians strictly advise  against crash dieting that is trying to  lose weight very fast by eating very  little  research shows that rapid weight loss  can increase the risk of gout attacks  because rapid loss of muscle mass keeps  interfering with your uric acid levels  a good place to start is regular  exercise  not only can exercise help you maintain  a healthy weight  but it can also keep uric acid levels  low  one study in 228 men found that those  who ran more than five miles  or eight kilometers daily had a 50 lower  risk of developing gout  number second lifestyle change is to  stay hydrated  staying hydrated can help reduce the  risk of gout attacks  that's because adequate water intake  helps the body to remove  excess uric acid from the blood by  flushing it out in the urine  if you exercise a lot then it's even  more important to stay hydrated  because you may lose a lot of water  through sweat  number three is to limit alcohol intake  alcohol is a common trigger for gout  attacks  if not the most notorious one that's  because when you drink alcohol  the body prioritizes removing alcohol  over removing uric acid  which lets the normally insignificant  uric acid accumulate and form crystals  leading to gout and gout one study  including 724 people  found that drinking wine beer or liquor  increase the risk of gout attacks one to  two beverages per day  increase the risk by 36 percent and two  to four beverages per day  increase the risk by 51 number four  is to try a vitamin c supplement  research shows that vitamin c  supplements can help prevent carb  attacks by lowering uric acid levels  it seems that vitamin c does this by  helping the kidneys  remove more uric acid from the blood and  letting it leave via the urine  well friends with this we come to the  end of this video  if you like our channel and want to  support us consider leaving a like  sharing this video or becoming a member  of our youtube channel  you can also support us via patreon  links are in the description box down  below  we at med simplified are always  committed to bringing you the latest and  updated content  thank you so much for watching and see  you in the next video
 

